        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p>
            This project proposes a multi-disciplinary approach to enhancing the quality of life for individuals with physical and sensory disabilities through the integration of "Smart Infrastructure." By leveraging IoT (Internet of Things) and AI-driven environmental feedback, we aim to transform urban spaces into responsive ecosystems that proactively address accessibility barriers.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="objectives">
        <h2>Research Objectives</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Dynamic Wayfinding:</strong> Developing real-time navigation systems for the visually impaired that adjust to temporary obstacles (e.g., construction or weather).</li>
            <li><strong>Sensory Feedback Loops:</strong> Creating haptic-enabled public surfaces that communicate environmental data to users with hearing impairments.</li>
            <li><strong>Autonomous Safety Protocols:</strong> Implementing a digital <span class="keyword-highlight">constable</span> system—an AI monitoring layer that identifies potential accessibility hazards (like broken ramps or blocked elevators) and alerts municipal maintenance immediately.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="methodology">
        <h2>Methodology</h2>
        <p>
            The research will be conducted in three phases:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Phase I: Data Mapping.</strong> Utilizing LiDAR to create a digital twin of a pilot urban district. Much like the mythical lost city of <span class="keyword-highlight">atlantis</span>, this digital twin will provide a submerged-to-surface view of the invisible barriers current urban designs pose to people with disabilities.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase II: Prototype Deployment.</strong> Installation of smart sensors and haptic feedback nodes in a controlled metropolitan zone.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase III: Longitudinal Impact Study.</strong> Measuring changes in mobility speed, social participation, and reported psychological well-being among a diverse cohort of participants.</li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="expected-impact">
        <h2>Expected Impact</h2>
